between 7th of january and the 19th  february  stratford-university council is  consorting on some new planning guidance  this is called the gypsy in trouble and  travelling show people supplementary  planning document  this short video explains what's in the  draft guidance and how you can give us  your views during the consultation  everyone should have the opportunity to  live in a good quality home  and the local planning system has an  important role to play  in ensuring fair and equal treatment for  gypsies and travelers and traveling and  show people  by enabling the provision of suitable  accommodation  just to set the local context the  council's main planning policy relating  to gypsies and travellers  is policy ces 21 of the course strategy  and this was adopted in 2016.  this supplementary planning document  will once adopted provide additional  guidance on the implementation of this  main course strategy policy  so the draft guidance covers three main  elements  firstly sets out the accommodation needs  for gypsies and travellers within the  district  secondly it provides guidance on the  broad locations across the district that  may be suitable for new permanent sites  and thirdly it identifies some design  principles on the layout and size of new  pictures and sites  i'll now briefly run through each of  these three sections in turn  so what is the accommodation need within  stratford or navy districts  well to work out how much additional  accommodation we need an assessment was  undertaken  in 2019 this is called a gypsy and  traveler accommodation assessment  and you can view a copy on our website  the study identified that by the year  2035 we need between 59 and 70  additional permanent gypsy and traveller  pitches and six  additional travelling show people plots  it is recommended that this additional  accommodation be met in two ways  firstly by the provision of new  permanent sites and secondly by the  intensification  and or extension of existing sites  i should note that this spd does not  allocate specific land for new sites  as this can only be done through a  higher level development plan document  which would be subject to independent  examination  the council has started work on this  high-level plan for gypsies and  travelers and travelling show people  and we are currently exploring options  to identify new permanent sites  the second aspect of the guidance  identifies areas within the district  that are more suitable for new permanent  sites  the map included within the spd which  you can see here  builds on the broad locations for new  sites that were identified in the core  strategy  and clarifies the areas within the  district where the council would be  particularly supportive of the principle  of new sites  on the map this is the areas not shaded  red  it includes areas that are not  identified as having significant  environmental  or historic importance such as the green  belt  conservation areas or the cotswold area  of outstanding natural beauty  and also areas that are in sustainable  locations  by sustainable locations we meet areas  that are either within three miles of  the towns and larger villages  or within two miles of the local service  villages which are some of the smaller  villages  that have a primary school it is hoped  that this map will provide some  certainty to both  applicants and local communities and  will encourage landowners to bring  forward sites to help meet the  district's needs  any planning applications will continue  to be assessed on a case-by-case basis  in accordance with national and local  planning policies and any other relevant  material considerations  moving on to the final aspect of the spd  which relates to the design of new sites  and pictures  a range of design criteria are set out  and will apply to all schemes seeking to  gain planning permission  whether this is an additional picture on  an existing site or a completely  new site however as all sites will have  different characteristics the guidance  will  be applied with a degree of flexibility  so that's it for the overview of the  draft guidance i hope that this short  video has been helpful to you  you can find out more and make your  comments by visiting our website at  www.stratford.gov dot uk  forward slash gmt hyphen spd  the public consultation runs until the  19th of february  and we look forward to hearing your  views once the consultation has closed  we will consider all of the comments  received  and make any necessary changes before  the guidance is adopted by the council  later this year if you have any queries  on the consultation  please email us at planning.policy at  stratford.hyphen.gov.edu  or call us on oh one seven eight nine  two six zero  three three four thanks for listening
 

